---
import PitchStretch from '../../components/demos/PitchStretch.tsx';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const playRate = `engine.sound('coin').play({
  pitch: 1.5,                         // 1.5× = chipmunk pitch + faster
});

// With jitter — varies per voice, makes stacked SFX feel organic:
engine.sound('hit').play({
  pitch: { jitter: 0.08 },           // ±8% playback rate
});`;

const stretch = `import { StretchProcessor } from '@schmooky/zvuk';

const original = await fetch('/music/bg.webm')
  .then((r) => r.arrayBuffer())
  .then((ab) => engine.context.decodeAudioData(ab));

// Render a slower copy at half-tempo, pitch unchanged.
const slowMo = StretchProcessor.stretchBuffer(engine.context, original, 2);

engine.createSound('bg-half', slowMo, { bus: 'music' });
engine.sound('bg-half').play({ loop: true });`;

const factory = `// Wrap it for re-use.
const stretched = (factor: number) =>
  StretchProcessor.stretchBuffer(engine.context, original, factor);

engine.createSound('bg-1.25', stretched(1.25), { bus: 'music' });
engine.createSound('bg-1.5',  stretched(1.5),  { bus: 'music' });
engine.createSound('bg-2.0',  stretched(2.0),  { bus: 'music' });`;

const worklet = `import { ensureStretchWorklet, createStretchWorkletNode } from '@schmooky/zvuk';

await ensureStretchWorklet(engine.context);
const node = createStretchWorkletNode(engine.context, { stretchFactor: 1 });

// Insert into a bus's FX chain — the stretch param is automatable.
engine.bus('music').addFx({
  input: node,
  output: node,
  bypassed: false,
  dispose: () => node.dispose(),
});

// Live varispeed ramp over 2 seconds. NOTE: the realtime worklet is NOT
// pitch-preserving — stretch < 1 plays slower AND drops pitch (tape-style).
node.stretch.linearRampToValueAtTime(0.5, engine.context.currentTime + 2);`;

const liveRate = `// Voice-level rate control (alters pitch + tempo, not pitch-preserving).
// Cheaper than the worklet, fine when you don't care about pitch.
const v = engine.sound('boss-stinger').play();
v.setPlaybackRate(0.6, { duration: 0.8, curve: 'easeOut' });`;
---
<Docs
  title="Pitch & time-stretch"
  description="Two ways to change speed. Cheap playback-rate moves pitch and tempo together; granular time-stretch holds the pitch."
  eyebrow="FX"
>

  <p>
    "Slow this down" means two completely different things depending on
    whether you also wanted it to drop a fifth in pitch. Tape does one.
    A DAW does the other. They cost about a thousand times different amounts.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cheap one is free: <code>AudioBufferSourceNode.playbackRate</code>
    finishes the buffer faster and raises the pitch to match, exactly like
    winding tape. At 1.5× a sample ends 50% sooner and sits a fifth higher.
    zvuk exposes it as <code>play({'{ pitch }'})</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The expensive one holds pitch while moving tempo.
    <a href="/api/StretchProcessor/"><code>StretchProcessor</code></a> is an
    offline granular renderer, SOLA-style, that takes one AudioBuffer and
    returns a stretched copy at the original pitch.
  </p>

  <h2>Live demo</h2>
  <p>
    Both modes on the same source. Move the slider and press both buttons.
    One sounds like a chipmunk. The other sounds like the same performance,
    slower.
  </p>
  <PitchStretch client:visible />

  <h2>Pitch via <code>play()</code>, which moves tempo too</h2>
  <Snippet code={playRate} lang="ts" />
  <p>
    Reach for this when a sound should feel smaller or bigger, when stacked
    one-shots need random variation so they don't machine-gun, or when you
    want an outright retro arcade effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Time-stretch via <code>StretchProcessor</code>, which holds pitch</h2>
  <Snippet code={stretch} lang="ts" />
  <p>
    Reach for this when music has to slow for a boss intro, when a loop needs
    doubling for a chase, or when vocal cadence has to match across takes
    recorded on different days.
  </p>

  <h3>Pre-render multiple speeds</h3>
  <p>
    The renderer is offline, so call it once per buffer and factor pair and
    cache what comes back. This is the right shape when you know the factors
    at load time and never need to ramp between them.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={factory} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Realtime varispeed via AudioWorklet</h3>
  <p>
    For a ramp that happens while audio is playing, use the worklet node
    instead. Boss intros that bend, gameplay slow motion. Its
    <code>stretch</code> parameter is a real <code>AudioParam</code>, so every
    standard automation method works on it.
  </p>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">The realtime worklet is varispeed, not pitch-preserving.</div>
    Unlike the offline <code>StretchProcessor</code>, this node resamples a ring
    buffer at a variable rate, so ramping <code>stretch</code> changes pitch
    <em>and</em> tempo together (tape-style). Use it when you want that effect;
    pre-render with <code>StretchProcessor</code> when you need the pitch held.
  </div>
  <Snippet code={worklet} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Voice-level rate ramp (cheap, alters pitch)</h3>
  <Snippet code={liveRate} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Quality notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>StretchProcessor</strong> uses overlap-add granular synthesis
      with cross-correlation alignment for stretch factors below 2.5×, plus a
      high-frequency-restore filter that compensates for the comb filtering
      granular methods introduce. Good for music and dialogue.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Past about 3×</strong> the granular artifacts are audible.
      Impressionistic ambience hides them. Clean dialogue does not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Factor &lt; 1</strong> (i.e. play <em>faster</em> while preserving
      pitch) is not currently supported. Use <code>play({'{ pitch }'})</code> instead
      and accept the pitch shift, or pre-render shorter versions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Don't time-stretch in a hot loop.</div>
    The processor is offline but synchronous. A one-second clip at 2× costs
    around 30 ms on a fast laptop, which is two dropped frames if you do it
    during gameplay. Pre-render at load time.
  </div>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Don't stretch SFX.</div>
    Transients under about 100 ms come out granular and strange. Use
    <code>play({'{ pitch }'})</code> for SFX and
    <code>StretchProcessor</code> for sustained sources.
  </div>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/voice/">Voice</a> covers pitch jitter at play time.</li>
    <li><a href="/fx/">FX index</a></li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
